#
# description : This .audit is designed against the CIS Oracle Linux 9 STIG Benchmark v1.0.0
#
#<ui_metadata>
#<display_name>CIS Oracle Linux 9 STIG v1.0.0 CAT I</display_name>
#<spec>
#  <type>CIS</type>
#  <name>Oracle Linux 9 STIG</name>
#  <profile>CAT I</profile>
#  <version>1.0.0</version>
#  <link>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024</link>
#</spec>
#<labels>agent,cis,disa,oracle_linux_9</labels>
#<benchmark_refs>800-53,800-53r5,CCI,LEVEL,Rule-ID,STIG-ID,Vuln-ID</benchmark_refs>
#<variables>
#  <variable>
#    <name>PLATFORM_VERSION</name>
#    <default>9</default>
#    <description>Linux Distribution Version</description>
#    <info>Linux Distribution Version</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#  <variable>
#    <name>FIPS_POLICY</name>
#    <default>FIPS</default>
#    <description>FIPS Crypto Policy and submodule</description>
#    <info>Valid values include FIPS, FIPS:AD-SUPPORT, FIPS:NO-ENFORCE-EMS</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#</variables>
#</ui_metadata>

<check_type:"Unix">

<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "Oracle Linux 9 platform check"
      file        : "/etc/system-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*Oracle[\\s]+Linux.*release"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*Oracle[\\s]+Linux.*release[\\s]+@PLATFORM_VERSION@"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "CIS_Oracle_Linux_9_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_I.audit from CIS Oracle Linux 9 STIG v1.0.0"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      show_output : YES
    </report>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
          description : "password-auth"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/password-auth"
          regex       : "^[^#]*nullok"
          expect      : "^[^#]*nullok"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
          description : "system-auth"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/system-auth"
          regex       : "^[^#]*nullok"
          expect      : "^[^#]*nullok"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.199 OL09-00-001110"
          info        : "OL 9 must not allow blank or null passwords.

GROUP ID: V-271634
RULE ID: SV-271634r1091614

If an account has an empty password, anyone could log in and run commands with the privileges of that account. Accounts with empty passwords should never be used in operational environments."
          solution    : "Remove any instances of the \"nullok\" option in the \"/etc/pam.d/password-auth\" and \"/etc/pam.d/system-auth\" files to prevent logons with empty passwords.

Note: Manual changes to the listed file may be overwritten by the \"authselect\" program."
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271634r1091614_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-001110,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271634"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "root, admin, or administrator"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*=[\\s]*\"(?i)(root|admin|administrator)(?-i)\"$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
          description : "1.21 OL09-00-000050"
          info        : "OL 9 must require a unique superuser's name upon booting into single-user and maintenance modes.

GROUP ID: V-271451
RULE ID: SV-271451r1137691

Having a nondefault grub superuser username makes password-guessing attacks less effective."
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to have a unique username for the grub superuser account.

Edit the \"/etc/grub.d/01_users\" file and add or modify the following lines in the \"### BEGIN /etc/grub.d/01_users ###\" section:

set superusers=\"superusers-account\"
export superusers

Once the superuser account has been added, update the grub.cfg file by running:

$ sudo grubby --update-kernel=ALL'"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271451r1137691_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-000050,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-271451"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*=[\\s]*\"(?i)(root|admin|administrator)(?-i)\"$"
        </custom_item>
      </then>

      <else>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "1.21 OL09-00-000050"
          info        : "OL 9 must require a unique superuser's name upon booting into single-user and maintenance modes.

GROUP ID: V-271451
RULE ID: SV-271451r1137691

Having a nondefault grub superuser username makes password-guessing attacks less effective.

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to have a unique username for the grub superuser account.

Edit the \"/etc/grub.d/01_users\" file and add or modify the following lines in the \"### BEGIN /etc/grub.d/01_users ###\" section:

set superusers=\"superusers-account\"
export superusers

Once the superuser account has been added, update the grub.cfg file by running:

$ sudo grubby --update-kernel=ALL'"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271451r1137691_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-000050,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-271451"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
          severity    : MEDIUM
        </custom_item>
      </else>
    </if>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "SELinux enabled"
          cmd         : "/sbin/getenforce"
          expect      : "^Enforcing$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "SELINUX enforcing config file"
          file        : "/etc/selinux/config"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*(?i)SELINUX(?-i)[\\s]*=[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*(?i)SELINUX(?-i)[\\s]*=[\\s]*enforcing[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.22 OL09-00-000060"
          info        : "OL 9 must use a Linux Security Module configured to enforce limits on system services.

GROUP ID: V-271452
RULE ID: SV-271452r1091068

Without verification of the security functions, security functions may not operate correctly and the failure may go unnoticed. Security function is defined as the hardware, software, and/or firmware of the information system responsible for enforcing the system security policy and supporting the isolation of code and data on which the protection is based. Security functionality includes, but is not limited to, establishing system accounts, configuring access authorizations (i.e., permissions, privileges), setting events to be audited, and setting intrusion detection parameters.

This requirement applies to operating systems performing security function verification/testing and/or systems and environments that require this functionality.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000445-GPOS-00199, SRG-OS-000134-GPOS-00068"
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to verify correct operation of security functions.

Edit the file \"/etc/selinux/config\" and add or modify the following line:

SELINUX=enforcing

A reboot is required for the changes to take effect."
          reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53|SI-6a.,800-53r5|SC-3,800-53r5|SI-6a.,CCI|CCI-001084,CCI|CCI-002696,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,ITSG-33|SI-6a.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-271452r1091068_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-000060,Vuln-ID|V-271452"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.24 OL09-00-000070"
      info        : "OL 9 must enable FIPS mode.

GROUP ID: V-271454
RULE ID: SV-271454r1092458

Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of using encryption to protect data. The operating system must implement cryptographic modules adhering to the higher standards approved by the federal government since this provides assurance they have been tested and validated. This includes NIST FIPS-validated cryptography for the following: Provisioning digital signatures, generating cryptographic hashes, and to protect data requiring data-at-rest protections in accordance with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000033-GPOS-00014, SRG-OS-000125-GPOS-00065, SRG-OS-000396-GPOS-00176, SRG-OS-000423-GPOS-00187, SRG-OS-000478-GPOS-00223"
      solution    : "Configure OL 9 to implement FIPS mode with the following command:

$ sudo fips-mode-setup --enable

Reboot the system for the changes to take effect."
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.13,800-171|3.7.5,800-171|3.13.8,800-171|3.13.11,800-171r3|03.07.05b.,800-171r3|03.13.08,800-171r3|03.13.11,800-53|AC-17(2),800-53|MA-4c.,800-53|SC-8,800-53|SC-13,800-53r5|AC-17(2),800-53r5|MA-4c.,800-53r5|SC-8,800-53r5|SC-13b.,CCI|CCI-000068,CCI|CCI-000877,CCI|CCI-002418,CCI|CCI-002450,CN-L3|7.1.2.7(g),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(d),CN-L3|8.1.2.2(a),CN-L3|8.1.2.2(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(a),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(c),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(d),CN-L3|8.5.2.2,CSF|PR.AC-3,CSF|PR.DS-2,CSF|PR.DS-5,CSF|PR.MA-2,CSF|PR.PT-4,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iv),HIPAA|164.312(e)(1),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(ii),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.14,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.6.7,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.24,ISO/IEC-27001|A.6.2.2,ISO/IEC-27001|A.10.1.1,ITSG-33|AC-17(2),ITSG-33|MA-4c.,ITSG-33|SC-8,ITSG-33|SC-8a.,ITSG-33|SC-13,ITSG-33|SC-13a.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M5.2.6,NESA|T2.3.4,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,NESA|T4.5.1,NESA|T4.5.2,NESA|T5.4.2,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T7.3.3,NESA|T7.4.1,NIAv2|AM37,NIAv2|CY3,NIAv2|CY4,NIAv2|CY5b,NIAv2|CY5c,NIAv2|CY5d,NIAv2|CY7,NIAv2|IE8,NIAv2|IE9,NIAv2|IE12,NIAv2|NS5e,NIAv2|NS29,NIAv2|SS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.3,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|4.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.7,PCI-DSSv4.0|4.2.1,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,Rule-ID|SV-271454r1092458_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-000070,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.6,TBA-FIISB|45.2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271454"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      cmd         : "/bin/fips-mode-setup --check"
      expect      : "^FIPS mode is enabled\\.$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "Check GDM installed"
          rpm         : "gdm-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "gt"
          required    : YES
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "1.254 OL09-00-002161"
          info        : "OL 9 must not allow unattended or automatic logon via the graphical user interface.

GROUP ID: V-271691
RULE ID: SV-271691r1091785

Failure to restrict system access to authenticated users negatively impacts operating system security."
          solution    : "Configure the GNOME desktop display manager to disable automatic login.

Set AutomaticLoginEnable to false in the [daemon] section in /etc/gdm/custom.conf. For example:

[daemon]
AutomaticLoginEnable=false"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271691r1091785_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002161,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271691"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          file        : "/etc/gdm/custom.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*(?i)AutomaticLoginEnable[\\s]*=[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*(?i)AutomaticLoginEnable[\\s]*=[\\s]*false[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </then>

      <else>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.254 OL09-00-002161"
          info        : "OL 9 must not allow unattended or automatic logon via the graphical user interface.

GROUP ID: V-271691
RULE ID: SV-271691r1091785

Failure to restrict system access to authenticated users negatively impacts operating system security."
          solution    : "Configure the GNOME desktop display manager to disable automatic login.

Set AutomaticLoginEnable to false in the [daemon] section in /etc/gdm/custom.conf. For example:

[daemon]
AutomaticLoginEnable=false"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271691r1091785_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002161,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271691"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
        </report>
      </else>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.268 OL09-00-002343"
      info        : "OL 9 SSHD must not allow blank passwords.

GROUP ID: V-271706
RULE ID: SV-271706r1091830

If an account has an empty password, anyone could log on and run commands with the privileges of that account. Accounts with empty passwords should never be used in operational environments."
      solution    : "Configure OL 9 to prevent SSH users from logging on with blank passwords by editing the following line in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\" or in a file in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d\":

PermitEmptyPasswords no

Restart the SSH daemon for the settings to take effect:

$ sudo systemctl restart sshd.service"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.3,800-171r3|03.05.03,800-53|IA-2(2),800-53r5|IA-2(2),CCI|CCI-000766,CN-L3|7.1.3.1(a),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(e),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(a),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2(2),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.4.2,NIAv2|AM2,NIAv2|AM8,NIAv2|AM14b,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271706r1091830_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002343,SWIFT-CSCv1|1.2,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-271706"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sshd -T | /bin/grep -i PermitEmptyPasswords"
      expect      : "(?i)[\\s]*PermitEmptyPasswords[\\s]+\"?no\"?[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.269 OL09-00-002344"
      info        : "OL 9 must enable the Pluggable Authentication Module (PAM) interface for SSHD.

GROUP ID: V-271707
RULE ID: SV-271707r1091833

When UsePAM is set to \"yes\", PAM runs through account and session types properly. This is important when restricted access to services based off of IP, time, or other factors of the account is needed. Additionally, this ensures users can inherit certain environment variables on login or disallow access to the server."
      solution    : "Configure the OL 9 SSHD to use the UsePAM interface by adding or modifying the following line in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\" or in a file in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d\".

UsePAM yes

Restart the SSH daemon for the settings to take effect:

$ sudo systemctl restart sshd.service"
      reference   : "800-171|3.7.5,800-171r3|03.07.05b.,800-53|MA-4c.,800-53r5|MA-4c.,CCI|CCI-000877,CSF|PR.MA-2,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|MA-4c.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T2.3.4,NESA|T5.4.4,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,Rule-ID|SV-271707r1091833_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002344,TBA-FIISB|45.2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271707"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sshd -T | /bin/grep -i UsePAM"
      expect      : "(?i)[\\s]*UsePAM[\\s]+\"?yes\"?[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "libreswan installed"
          rpm         : "libreswan-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "gt"
          required    : YES
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description     : "1.306 OL09-00-002404"
          info            : "OL 9 IP tunnels must use 140-3 approved cryptographic algorithms.

GROUP ID: V-271743
RULE ID: SV-271743r1184204

Overriding the system crypto policy makes the behavior of the Libreswan service violate expectations and makes system configuration more fragmented."
          solution        : "Configure IPsec to use the systemwide cryptographic policy.

Add the following line to \"/etc/ipsec.conf\":

include /etc/crypto-policies/back-ends/libreswan.config"
